@title Fathom Bank Product Design Principles
@subtitle Design Systems team, for all customer-facing apps

These principles were agreed in a workshop with 26 designers and engineers on 2026-09-17. They apply to every customer-facing app at Fathom Bank. Link to this page from every design review template.

## Clarity over cleverness

Customers should understand what a control does before they touch it. Plain labels beat clever icons, especially where money is involved.

- Do: label buttons with the action, such as "Send transfer".
- Avoid: icons without text for money movement.

## Show the money

Fees, limits and totals belong in front of the customer before they commit. Surprises at the end of a flow erode trust faster than any single fee.

- Do: show fees before confirmation.
- Avoid: revealing fees on the final screen.

## Forgive mistakes

People make mistakes, especially under time pressure. Our products should make recovery easy and quick.

- Do: allow undo for 10 seconds after a transfer is scheduled.
- Avoid: making cancellation a phone call.

## Accessible by default

Accessibility is part of the default design, not an add-on. Every screen should work for customers who cannot see color or who rely on assistive technology.

- Do: meet a contrast ratio of 4.5 to 1.
- Avoid: relying on color alone for status.

## Calm, not urgent

Financial decisions deserve time. Our interfaces should state deadlines plainly and let customers choose when to act.

- Do: state plain deadlines.
- Avoid: countdown timers that push decisions.

## Explain the why

A customer who understands why something happened can decide what to do next. When we hold a payment, the reason and the next step belong on the screen, not in a support queue.

- Do: explain that a payment is on hold for a security check and when it will clear.
- Avoid: showing "Payment on hold" with no reason or next step.

> If a customer has to read it twice, we wrote it wrong.
> — Ines Albrecht, Head of Design

! Disagreements with a principle go to the monthly design council, not to individual design reviews.
